  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1219 AM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nA weak vort lobe will separate from a northeastern Pacific low, \ncutting off into a dry low that will cross southern CA into the Baja \non Thursday. Other than assisting with cirrus production over NM, \nthis system will not pose much of any sensible impact to the weather \nwith tranquility persisting Thursday. The low will get steered \nfarther eastward by the subtropical jet into the Mexican mainland on \nFriday. This will introduce a more meridional, and slightly stiffer, \ncomponent to the flow aloft on Friday. The central mountain chain \n(and specifically the highlands around Clines Corners) would turn \nbreezy to windy Friday with the 700 mb flow of 20-30 kt also \nsuggesting breeziness in additional zones stretching northwestward \nto the Four Corners. \n\nThe flow aloft is advertised to increase more into the weekend, \nshifting from a northwesterly to a zonal direction. This will lend \nthe pattern to more potential progressive perturbations going \nfarther into next week. As it stands right now, an increase in winds \nlooks more probable than any introduction of additional \nprecipitation to the forecast, even by the operational 00Z ECMWF \nsolution that shows a more stalwart shortwave sliding across the \ncentral Rockies and into the central to southern plains on Saturday. \n The next incoming shortwave, slated to cross NM on Monday, also \nappears dry, but the strengthening gradient aloft would bring a \nboost to wind speeds. Temperatures look to hold above normal through \nthe weekend into early next week with daytime highs exceeding normal \nby 5 to 10 degrees in most locations.",
